thinking:
Now I understand the flags:

- `MMCSD_DATAXFR` = `(1 << 10)` = 0x400 — indicates a data transfer (read)
- `MMCSD_WRXFR` = `(4 << 10)` = 0x1000 — write direction
- `MMCSD_WRDATAXFR` = `MMCSD_DATAXFR | MMCSD_WRXFR` = 0x1400 — write data transfer
- `MMCSD_RDDATAXFR` = `MMCSD_DATAXFR` = 0x400 — read data transfer

So for a READ operation, the flags would be `MMCSD_DATAXFR` (bit 10 set, bit 12 clear). For a WRITE, both bits 10 and 12 are set.

But the log shows `rw=1`, which means our code is setting `s_cmd.rw = 1`. This happens when `cmd & MMCSD_WRDATAXFR` is true. But `MMCSD_WRDATAXFR = 0x1400`. For a read, the flags should be `0x400` (just `MMCSD_DATAXFR`), which would NOT match `MMCSD_WRDATAXFR`.

So either:
1. The command value has both `MMCSD_DATAXFR` and `MMCSD_WRXFR` set (meaning write)
2. Or our check is wrong

Let me check our code:
```c
if (cmd & MMCSD_DATAXFR)
  {
    s_cmd.data_expected = 1;
    if (cmd & MMCSD_WRDATAXFR)
      {
        s_cmd.rw = 1;
      }
  }
```

Wait, `cmd & MMCSD_WRDATAXFR` checks if ANY of the bits in `MMCSD_WRDATAXFR` are set. Since `MMCSD_WRDATAXFR = MMCSD_DATAXFR | MMCSD_WRXFR`, and `MMCSD_DATAXFR` is set for reads, the condition `cmd & MMCSD_WRDATAXFR` will be true for BOTH reads and writes!

The fix should be: check specifically for `MMCSD_WRXFR` bit:
```c
if (cmd & MMCSD_WRXFR)
  {
    s_cmd.rw = 1;
  }
```

This way, `rw` is only set when the write direction bit is explicitly set.

